  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">A transcript records what was said. The minutes record what was decided.</p>
    <p>
      On 18 May 2026 the district council of Konstanz met for three and a half hours. Afterwards the district
      office wrote the official minutes, as after every meeting: every resolution with its vote, the statements
      that belong on record, the facts of the meeting, who was present, who left the room and when, and who has to
      sign. The transcription the office had commissioned delivers the words. The minutes were still written in
      Word, by listening again and checking every resolution against a separate resolution record.
    </p>
    <p>
      I care about this kind of problem because it is the one Levelwork works on: turning a conversation into a record
      that people can check and sign off.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">I followed the minutes from the end of the meeting to the signature, then built the editor for it.</p>
    <p>
      With the office I went through how they really work after a meeting, step by step, until the signed minutes. From
      that I designed and built the online editor that turns the verbatim record, the corrected transcript, into the
      minutes. We had been commissioned for the verbatim record. Working with the office, we saw that the minutes matter
      far more, so we moved the focus there, and I designed the minutes editor. I built it with Claude Code.
    </p>
    <p>
      For every contribution the office decides how it goes into the minutes: edit it, keep it verbatim, put it into
      indirect speech, replace it with a standard text, or leave it out. The resolution record and the office's template
      are uploaded, and the editor takes over the resolutions with their votes, attendance, start and end and the
      signatures, word for word. The office corrects inside the tool instead of in Word and exports to Word or PDF.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How we tested it</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">One real meeting, all real documents, and an export nobody touched.</p>
    <p>
      On 15 October I ran the public council meeting of 18 May 2026 through the editor with everything the office uses:
      the record of the 2 h 39 min recording, the resolution record and the office's template. I left the export
      unedited and compared it with what the office needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The resolutions came through complete and word for word, with votes and numbering, and so did the header, the
      agenda, attendance and signatures. But speech landed under the wrong agenda item whenever the chair did not
      announce “agenda item 14” in so many words, and the recording ended in the middle of item 19 without the export
      saying so. Since then the editor assigns speech to agenda items from the meeting documents, and a check view shows
      every item that has no speech.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="minutes-agenda" ratio="1200 / 460" narrow="600 / 680" />
      <figcaption>
        In the first run, speech landed under the wrong item whenever the chair did not announce it, and the export did
        not say where the recording ended. Now the items come from the meeting documents, and every item without speech
        is flagged.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div><dt>37</dt><dd>resolutions with their votes, taken over word for word</dd></div>
      <div><dt>9</dt><dd>agenda items without speech in the first run, now flagged</dd></div>
      <div><dt>17,000</dt><dd>words, roughly, in the record of one meeting</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Choices instead of typing, documents instead of retyping, and the page always in view.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>A choice per contribution.</strong> A free text editor would have been quicker to build. But the office
      does not rewrite the meeting; for each contribution it decides what the minutes need. Five choices make that
      decision fast and leave a trace of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Read the documents, say how sure we are.</strong> The resolution record arrives as Word or PDF. The editor
      reads both, shows what it found, and warns when a PDF had to be reconstructed from the page layout.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The minutes stay in view.</strong> Editing and preview scroll together, so the office always sees the
      page that will be signed, not a form that becomes a page later.
    </p>
  </section>

